# The coverage exclusion list. ONE file, in this repository, for the one
# measurement this repository excludes code from.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#
# An entry permits ONE path prefix to be left out of the `go tool cover` total the
# `gate` job compares against its floor. Without it, a path either is measured
# (which is the default and the only safe default) or somebody edits a threshold,
# and the second is how a coverage floor stops being a coverage floor.
#
# It is read by `bin/coverage-floor`, which the `coverage` step of
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs, and it is validated by
# `TestTheCoverageExclusionIsOnlyGeneratedCode` in `internal/platform/ci`, which is
# the only thing that makes it a declaration rather than a comment.
#
# WHY IT IS DECLARED AND NEVER INFERRED
#
# The obvious alternative is to exclude every file whose header says
# `// Code generated … DO NOT EDIT.` That fails in the dangerous direction. The
# header is written by whichever generator ran: a generator that stops writing it,
# or a `//go:generate` line pointed at a different tool, SILENTLY RE-EXCLUDES a
# quarter of this module while the floor stays where it is, and the build goes
# green having measured almost nothing. An inferred exclusion that fails toward
# MORE coverage is safe — the floor bites and somebody is told. One that fails
# toward LESS coverage is not, and this is the one that would.
#
# So the path is written here, reviewed, and the test walks the tree to check that
# what it names is still what it said it named.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE ONLY EXCLUSION IN THE REPOSITORY
#
# `.golangci.yml` carries a second one, for the same file, in the same shape: a
# checked-in anchored path with a test that walks every `.go` file and fails if it
# matched one more. `client/lint_exclusion_test.go` holds that one. Two
# exclusions, one discipline, two tests — because a lint exclusion and a coverage
# exclusion are read by different tools and there is no single place both can
# live, and inventing a shared format to hold two entries would be a second
# dialect rather than one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR HYGIENE RULES
#
# These are kit's `templates/tier/skip-allowlist` rules, not new ones. An
# allowlist that invents its own dialect is an allowlist nobody learns twice.
#
# 1. EVERY ENTRY NAMES A REASON.
#    Without one, "generated" becomes the reason for everything, including the
#    hand-written file somebody put in a generated directory.
#
# 2. EVERY ENTRY NAMES AN OWNER.
#    An exclusion nobody owns is an exclusion nobody will ever remove.
#
# 3. EVERY ENTRY HAS BOTH DATES, AND THE GATE READS THE CLOCK.
#    `since` dates the decision. `until` is the ratchet: an expired entry is a
#    FAILURE on the day it passes, not a warning. `bin/coverage-floor` exits
#    non-zero on an expired entry, and the test says so too — so this file is
#    expected to go red roughly once, and the correct response is to delete the
#    entry or move the date with a new reason.
#    `until` here is not a formality. MD16 says the correct long-term shape is a
#    generated SDK in its OWN Go module, with its own near-zero floor, because a
#    separate module makes the boundary a compile-time fact rather than a config
#    entry — and it says that decision was deferred because it changes the import
#    path of a published client. This expiry is that deferral turning into a
#    date: it comes due before a second repository asks for a generated client,
#    which is the signal MD16 names for revisiting rather than copying.
#
# 4. AN ENTRY THAT MATCHES NOTHING IS A FAILURE.
#    This is ESLint's `reportUnusedDisableDirectives` — a disable comment that no
#    longer suppresses anything — and it is the whole defence of the other three.
#    Without it this file is a ratchet that only turns one way: a generator that
#    moves leaves the entry behind, the entry stops matching, and the next person
#    adds another entry next to it.
#
# THE TOTAL IS PRINTED ON PASS, PROMINENTLY.
#
# Every entry individually looks justified. The aggregate is the problem, and an
# aggregate nobody is shown is an aggregate nobody watches — which is a green run
# is exactly where a growing list is least likely to be noticed. `bin/coverage-floor`
# prints it on every run, pass or fail, for the same reason kit does.
#
# `files=` AND `lines=` ARE NOT DECORATION
#
# They are the fingerprint of the excluded SET, and they are what makes "the
# excluded set changed" a failure rather than a silent change of denominator.
# Go's coverage step takes a package pattern and not a file, so this exclusion is
# necessarily a DIRECTORY and necessarily coarser than the lint exclusion beside
# it. The cost of that coarseness is paid here: a file added to an excluded
# directory changes `files=`/`lines=`, and the test fails until this line is
# rewritten. That is the moment somebody has to look at the new file and decide
# whether it is generated — which is the decision a bare directory exclusion
# would have made for them.
#
# `coverage-fail-under=` IS ON THE ENTRY, NOT IN A HEADER, ON PURPOSE
#
# The floor, the path and the size of the excluded set are ONE EDITABLE UNIT, and
# changing the denominator without restating the floor is the failure this file
# exists to prevent. `TestTheCoverageFloorInTheDeclarationIsTheFloorsFloor` reads
# this line and fails if it disagrees with the floor in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`
# or with the floor passed to kit — so "70%" cannot quietly come to mean three
# different things in three files.
#
# NOTE WHAT IS *NOT* HERE, and why
#
# There is no `measured=` field. The measured percentage is printed by
# `bin/coverage-floor` on every run, beside this file's contents and the floor —
# which is where MD16 says the floor's meaning has to be restated: "the measured
# number, the excluded set, and the floor are three facts that belong in one place
# and are checked together." A number written down by hand in a config file is the
# one fact of the three that cannot be checked, because it is a copy of a
# measurement rather than the measurement. The date-stamped "73.2% measured" in
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` is exactly that hazard and this packet removes it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
#
#   excluded coverage <path-prefix> files=<n> lines=<n> coverage-fail-under=<n> \
#       reason="…" owner=… since=YYYY-MM-DD until=YYYY-MM-DD
#
# `<path-prefix>` is a repository-relative directory, forward slashes, no `./`,
# no trailing slash. `reason` is quoted; everything else is bare. A `#` starts a
# comment. Blank lines are ignored. ONE entry per line: a wrapped reason is two
# entries, one of which is a parse error.
#
# The parser is `bin/coverage-floor`, and it fails closed on any line it does not
# understand — a reader that finds nothing agrees with a file that excludes
# nothing, and the two are indistinguishable in a log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

